WEBVTT

00:11.857 --> 00:14.563
[♪ upbeat music plays]

00:17.100 --> 00:18.670
Is this mine or yours?

00:18.671 --> 00:20.207
That’s actually yours,
but I steal it a lot.

00:20.407 --> 00:21.744
- [chuckles]
- Okay.

00:22.277 --> 00:23.313
Have you guys seen my phone?

00:23.479 --> 00:24.917
- Of course not.
- Always no.

00:25.183 --> 00:27.522
Issa, I got you a phone lanyard
for exactly this reason.

00:27.523 --> 00:30.127
Oh. Well, then has anyone seen
my phone lanyard?

00:30.260 --> 00:32.063
- [both scoff]
- Again, no.

00:32.064 --> 00:33.801
[groans]
I’m just gonna do the thing.

00:34.102 --> 00:37.040
[phone ringing]

00:39.546 --> 00:42.384
Yeah. [hums]

00:43.588 --> 00:47.060
- [hums] Okay.
- [Anton] Mmm.

00:49.566 --> 00:52.204
- [vocalizing]
- [beatboxing]

00:52.205 --> 00:53.840
- [grunting]
- [Anton] Whoo!

00:53.841 --> 00:56.045
Look at that, look at this,
look at this, watch this.

00:56.245 --> 00:57.582
[phone continues ringing]

00:58.250 --> 01:00.220
Yeah! I found it!

01:00.320 --> 01:02.357
[Anton, singsongy]
Okay, okay. Right there.

01:02.358 --> 01:03.961
- Don’t stop.
- [Samir beatboxing]

01:04.128 --> 01:05.229
My grandpa had a heart attack.

01:05.230 --> 01:06.498
- [gasps] What?
- [Anton] What?

01:06.499 --> 01:08.336
- Oh, God. Oh, shit.
- [Billie] What?

01:09.405 --> 01:12.545
Oh. Wait, this isn’t
my phone. [chuckling]

01:18.189 --> 01:19.325
Billie...

01:20.460 --> 01:21.963
I found your phone.

01:21.964 --> 01:26.472
[Samir inhales sharply]
Issa, Jesus Christ. [grunts]

01:27.374 --> 01:29.111
- Oh, Bills.
- [Anton] It’s gonna be okay.

01:29.112 --> 01:31.048
- Billie, it’s gonna be
okay, okay?
- [Samir] Yeah.

01:31.049 --> 01:34.789
- [taps]
- [phone ringing]

01:34.790 --> 01:36.392
[♪ "Bad Advice" playing]

01:53.928 --> 01:56.265
[♪ funky music plays]

01:58.537 --> 02:00.975
[reporter] More than 50,000
runners from around the world

02:01.142 --> 02:03.278
are taking on 26.2 miles...

02:03.279 --> 02:04.917
- [groans] Where’s the drama?
- I know.

02:05.183 --> 02:07.387
At a certain point, give us
an obstacle. Set a bear free.

02:07.388 --> 02:08.490
- Oh!
- See what happens.

02:08.858 --> 02:10.962
- [Issa] Great idea.
- Okay, be honest.

02:11.162 --> 02:12.664
Does this blazer work,

02:12.665 --> 02:14.168
or do I look like a divorcée
fighting for full custody?

02:14.268 --> 02:15.572
Full custody,
no visitation rights.

02:15.671 --> 02:17.507
- Fuck, I knew it.
- Billie, it’s a Sunday.

02:17.508 --> 02:18.943
They can’t make you go
into the newsroom on a Sunday.

02:18.944 --> 02:21.916
Make me? Sundays are
when PAs get ahead.

02:21.917 --> 02:24.589
I’m about to make
the supply closet my bitch.

02:24.590 --> 02:26.794
Hey, when you leave, can you
please head out the back?

02:26.893 --> 02:28.897
You see that caution tape
I put up?

02:28.898 --> 02:30.935
It was way too close a call
with that Postmate
the other night.

02:31.168 --> 02:33.238
My dad got
a Ring cam notification
that said "motion detected."

02:33.239 --> 02:35.545
Or you could just, hear me out,
tell your parents
that we’re living here.

02:35.711 --> 02:37.214
Mmm, that’s a great idea.

02:37.314 --> 02:39.518
Or you could just hear me out
while I say no.

02:39.519 --> 02:41.188
Okay, respectfully,

02:41.189 --> 02:43.526
you are hiding
three 24-year-olds
from a doorbell camera.

02:43.527 --> 02:46.466
Okay, my parents don’t even
like it when the guests use
the good towels.

02:46.566 --> 02:48.402
Trust me, honesty is not
the best policy here.

02:48.403 --> 02:51.042
Honesty is always
the best policy.

02:51.275 --> 02:52.244
[Issa] Mmm.

02:52.377 --> 02:54.380
- [whispering] Tell them.
- Okay.

02:54.381 --> 02:57.153
Billie won the Anne Frank
Student of Integrity Award
in the eighth grade.

02:57.154 --> 02:58.623
Oh, wow.

02:58.624 --> 03:00.160
Yeah, I told the principal
which kids drank.

03:00.427 --> 03:02.497
- Mmm, just like Anne would.
- Okay, I’m with Samir.

03:02.498 --> 03:04.968
So what if the man lies
to his sweet, retired parents

03:04.969 --> 03:05.002
So what if the man lies
to his sweet, retired parents
while they travel the country?

03:05.003 --> 03:06.973
while they travel the country?

03:06.974 --> 03:08.611
Right? Okay, please be careful
with the dumplings
on the couch.

03:08.777 --> 03:10.479
- Oh.
- I’m being careful.

03:10.480 --> 03:13.118
Lying is only a problem
if you’re bad at it, right?

03:13.119 --> 03:14.957
If you’re good at it,
you’re basically an author.

03:15.223 --> 03:18.531
You’re telling tales,
you’re weaving fables.

03:18.630 --> 03:20.167
Yeah, but I feel
like I never see you lie.

03:20.400 --> 03:22.038
- Really?
- Mm-hmm.

03:22.237 --> 03:24.041
- I’ve lied to you
like three times today.
- What? About what?

03:24.241 --> 03:25.878
Hmm. I can’t recall.

03:26.178 --> 03:27.815
- Ugh, I love that sweater.
- [gasps]

03:27.816 --> 03:30.386
- [chuckling] Thanks. Me too.
- Mm-hmm. [mouthing words]

03:30.387 --> 03:32.691
- [Samir] Anton?
- [presenter speaking on TV]

03:32.692 --> 03:34.595
- What?
- [Samir] Look.

03:34.596 --> 03:35.864
Nearly two million spectators
have lined the course today...

03:35.865 --> 03:37.601
- [Anton] What?
- [Issa] Oh, my God!

03:37.602 --> 03:39.003
- [Billie] Wait, what is that?
- [objects clatter]

03:39.004 --> 03:41.141
- Shit. [sighs]
- [Billie] Is that you?

03:41.743 --> 03:43.981
- [Anton] No, no, no.
- [Billie] "We love you,
Anton"? What--

03:44.248 --> 03:46.685
- Enhance. Samir, enhance.
- Th-Th-That’s not a thing.

03:46.686 --> 03:50.360
[Issa] "Our coworker is faster
than your coworker"? What...

03:50.695 --> 03:52.666
- [Anton breathing heavily]
- [Billie] Anton.

03:53.099 --> 03:57.775
So, I, um... I may have gotten
caught in a lie.

04:00.246 --> 04:01.650
[gasps]

04:02.586 --> 04:04.454
It’s like the wettest dumpling
of all time.

04:04.455 --> 04:06.392
So, how exactly
did this happen?

04:06.492 --> 04:08.328
[grunts] It’s just
a huge misunderstanding.

04:08.329 --> 04:11.602
- [Billie] Hmm. Mmm.
- Mainly due
to my behaviors and actions.

04:11.603 --> 04:13.840
- That’s the worst.
- [Anton] Okay, so last year,

04:13.841 --> 04:15.910
we did this icebreaker at work,
where everyone had to go around

04:15.911 --> 04:17.881
and say their name,
their hometown and their hobby.

04:17.882 --> 04:20.554
[gasps] But you have
no hobbies. Nor passions
nor pastimes.

04:20.555 --> 04:23.292
- I know.
- [Issa] So, what’d you say?

04:23.293 --> 04:25.063
So I panicked
and I said running.

04:25.296 --> 04:26.298
Wait, you run?

04:26.299 --> 04:28.971
- I have ran.
- You have run.

04:29.271 --> 04:30.440
He’s not even familiar
with the verb.

04:30.574 --> 04:31.944
It was supposed to be
a one-off lie,

04:32.043 --> 04:34.214
but you know
how coworkers are, right?

04:34.215 --> 04:36.485
You give them one fun fact,
and then they just,
like, latch onto it.

04:36.653 --> 04:38.591
I know, they’re vampires.

04:38.757 --> 04:41.361
So, I-I told them I was running
a 5K, and then I told them
I was running a 10K,

04:41.362 --> 04:45.538
and then I, um... I may have
mentioned that I was running
the marathon this year.

04:45.738 --> 04:48.275
- [Samir] Oh, my...
- For breast cancer awareness.
Don’t gasp.

04:48.276 --> 04:49.745
- Don’t do it.
- [gasps, coughs]

04:49.746 --> 04:51.950
What? I-I didn’t know
they’d show up.

04:52.050 --> 04:53.787
[scoffs] It’s
a hybrid remote job.

04:53.788 --> 04:55.657
Oh, God, you made
a web of lies.

04:55.658 --> 04:57.862
I get it. It’s like when I told
everyone I was born deaf.

04:57.863 --> 04:59.965
- You weren’t? What?
- [gasps]

04:59.966 --> 05:02.871
- What... Okay.
- Anyway, you just have
to come clean.

05:02.872 --> 05:04.007
[Anton scoffs]

05:05.043 --> 05:06.145
Or...

05:06.980 --> 05:09.818
Or... Or... I like "or."
Let’s explore "or."

05:09.819 --> 05:14.862
Or you could just weave
more web.

05:14.863 --> 05:16.165
[Issa gasps]

05:16.432 --> 05:17.502
- Okay, okay, let’s do it.
- [Billie] Mm-hmm.

05:17.702 --> 05:19.070
How would that work?

05:19.071 --> 05:22.846
So, we know where
your coworkers are waiting.

05:23.012 --> 05:24.715
- Right, right, right.
- Right?

05:24.716 --> 05:26.218
So you don’t actually
have to run the marathon.

05:26.485 --> 05:28.791
You just have to run
one singular block past them,

05:28.924 --> 05:30.694
and the web
of lies stays intact.

05:30.695 --> 05:32.731
What happened to the Anne
Frank Student of Integrity?

05:32.732 --> 05:34.536
I am honest
in my own life, okay?

05:34.702 --> 05:37.207
But we have a responsibility
to help our friend Anton
out of this.

05:37.474 --> 05:39.010
- Yeah.
- Okay, but after this,
no more lying.

05:39.011 --> 05:39.044
- Yeah.
- Okay, but after this,
no more lying.
[Anton] No more lying, promise.

05:39.045 --> 05:40.480
[Anton] No more lying, promise.

05:40.581 --> 05:42.049
Okay, let’s do this.
Should we go now?

05:42.050 --> 05:43.653
Okay, wait, we should wait,
like, three hours

05:43.654 --> 05:45.324
- so it’s a more realistic pace.
- Got it.

05:45.891 --> 05:47.360
Let’s call it two.

05:47.628 --> 05:49.365
- I’ve been fake training
a lot this year.
- [scoffs]

05:49.599 --> 05:52.002
- You guys...
- Okay. Um,
I’m gonna go color-code this.

05:52.003 --> 05:54.743
- [Issa] Ooh!
- [sighs] Look at her go,
our anal queen.

05:54.909 --> 05:56.647
And you just named our boat.

06:01.790 --> 06:03.594
- [patrons chattering]
- [blows ra I can’t think of a pun
for my sign.

06:03.727 --> 06:06.264
Pun? Mine just says,
"Run, slut."

06:06.265 --> 06:07.468
It does.

06:07.602 --> 06:10.139
- [phone beeps]
- [groans] Steve.

06:10.140 --> 06:11.542
[Samir] What?

06:11.543 --> 06:13.245
[sighs] Steve sent me
an apology Cameo.

06:13.479 --> 06:15.551
Issa, you’ve been seeing this
guy for, like, three weeks.

06:15.651 --> 06:17.287
He’s already
on his second apology Cameo.

06:17.555 --> 06:19.825
It’s not his fault that
he forgot my birthday, Sammy.

06:19.826 --> 06:21.228
He has ADHD.

06:21.229 --> 06:23.567
Buying a card is like
scaling a mountain for him.

06:23.568 --> 06:25.538
Okay, whatever.

06:25.738 --> 06:27.575
[groans, inhales deeply]

06:28.175 --> 06:31.817
Do I just attract shitty guys?
Like, is it me?

06:31.950 --> 06:33.386
I mean,
obviously it’s not me, but--

06:33.553 --> 06:34.888
- No, it’s you.
- What the hell?

06:34.889 --> 06:36.559
- Sammy?
- Yeah. Sorry.

06:36.660 --> 06:38.228
If you wanted a pep talk,
you could go to Billie,

06:38.229 --> 06:39.798
but you come to me,

06:39.799 --> 06:41.903
you’re gonna get the truth
and pocket Sour Straws.

06:41.904 --> 06:43.707
Okay, fine. What is the truth?

06:43.907 --> 06:45.443
- The truth is...
- [sighs]

06:45.711 --> 06:47.914
...you take in these, like,
strays, and then you’re shocked

06:47.915 --> 06:50.386
every time they turn around
and piss all over your leg.

06:50.521 --> 06:52.824
Remember Andy,
the street magician?

06:52.825 --> 06:55.330
- He was
an unhoused magician, actually.
- Okay.

06:55.597 --> 06:58.001
What about that guy who went
to NYU for freestyle rap?

06:58.002 --> 07:01.007
Oh, God. He tried to rhyme
everything with "bitch."

07:01.008 --> 07:03.145
Itch, ditch, rich.

07:03.146 --> 07:04.882
He’s always just
working his way up to "bitch."

07:05.718 --> 07:06.820
Straw?

07:08.456 --> 07:09.758
You know what?

07:09.759 --> 07:12.029
- You’re right. Fine. I’m done.
- Mm-hmm.

07:12.030 --> 07:16.205
I’m done fixing these lost,
broken men.

07:16.438 --> 07:18.710
- Thank you.
- It’s charity.
I’m not doing it anymore.

07:18.711 --> 07:20.581
I should get to be
the lost and broken one.

07:20.681 --> 07:22.017
- I’d be so good at it.
- Sorry. Excuse me.

07:22.117 --> 07:24.220
Can I borrow your bathroom?
Can I just...

07:24.221 --> 07:26.892
I’d like a glass
of water, please.
I feel like I need some water.

07:26.893 --> 07:29.665
[screams] Nobody touch him!
I can save him!

07:29.666 --> 07:31.636
- Issa.
- [Issa grunts, panting]

07:31.637 --> 07:32.738
Are you a doctor?

07:32.739 --> 07:33.807
Uh, dance teacher.

07:34.007 --> 07:35.108
Why? Do you not feel well?

07:35.109 --> 07:37.046
Can I just get
some water, please?

07:37.047 --> 07:39.719
Yes, um, could we please get
a water and a Diet Coke?

07:39.886 --> 07:40.854
- Oh. Okay.
- Thank you.

07:41.055 --> 07:43.894
- Hi. [panting]
- Hi.

07:45.263 --> 07:47.869
- You’re pretty.
- You too.

07:48.236 --> 07:49.840
[both chuckle]

07:49.939 --> 07:52.343
I just wanted
to check on my chicken fingers.

07:52.344 --> 07:53.412
[waiter] Sorry.

07:53.413 --> 07:54.883
[♪ upbeat music plays]

07:55.016 --> 07:56.787
[clerk] If you need it
by Monday,

07:56.886 --> 07:59.124
- there’s a Monday surcharge.
- [door opens]

07:59.692 --> 08:00.761
- Hi.
- Hey.

08:00.894 --> 08:02.264
Look, Billie made me
a fake bib.

08:02.497 --> 08:04.133
[Samir] Wow.

08:04.134 --> 08:06.506
Yeah, just don’t embarrass me,
okay? Kind of a big deal here.

08:07.307 --> 08:08.643
- [clerk] Hey!
- Okay.

08:08.744 --> 08:10.514
- [Billie] Hi, guys.
- Billie. Billie, Billie.

08:10.647 --> 08:12.016
- Customer of the century.
- [chuckles]

08:12.183 --> 08:14.254
♪ The karaoke queen ♪

08:14.255 --> 08:16.959
How much time does she spend
at this print shop?

08:16.960 --> 08:19.330
Yo, Billie’s here.
Billie’s here. Look.

08:19.331 --> 08:20.466
[clerks] ♪ Oh, home ♪

08:20.467 --> 08:22.002
Rob, it’s your day off.

08:22.003 --> 08:24.141
- ♪ Let me go home ♪
- ♪ Home ♪

08:24.274 --> 08:26.312
[all] ♪ Home is wherever... ♪

08:26.513 --> 08:28.149
- I think we have to kill her.
- Yeah, I think
it’d be a mercy kill.

08:28.249 --> 08:29.851
- ♪ Oh, home ♪
- [vocalizing]

08:29.852 --> 08:32.891
♪ Let me go home ♪

08:32.892 --> 08:36.232
♪ Home is
Wherever I’m with you ♪

08:36.533 --> 08:39.572
[singsongy] Billie, when are we
doing karaoke again?

08:39.573 --> 08:42.276
- [Billie] Name a day.
- Yeah, anything
you want printed.

08:42.277 --> 08:42.310
- [Billie] Name a day.
- Yeah, anything
you want printed.
Hannah, you are a lifesaver.

08:42.311 --> 08:44.616
Hannah, you are a lifesaver.

08:44.782 --> 08:48.355
And you need to tell us
when your next NewsNight piece
is gonna air, girl.

08:48.356 --> 08:49.992
- We’ve been tuning in.
- [Billie laughs]

08:49.993 --> 08:52.799
No one believes us
that we are friends

08:52.998 --> 08:55.337
with a whole-ass news reporter.

08:55.571 --> 08:58.208
- She does the news, y’all.
You knew that?
- What?

08:58.209 --> 09:00.346
- Y’all know Billie?
Y’all know Big Bill?
- [Anton] We know her.

09:00.647 --> 09:03.084
We’re with the reporter.
The whole-ass news--

09:03.085 --> 09:05.390
Who is the news reporter
that they’re speaking of?

09:05.557 --> 09:08.428
’Cause I know the very
truthful PA would never lie
about something like that.

09:08.429 --> 09:10.534
Yeah, what would Anne Frank
think of something like that?

09:10.801 --> 09:12.738
- [Anton] Huh.
- Okay, after this,
no more lying.

09:12.938 --> 09:14.274
- Okay.
- Okay.

09:14.275 --> 09:16.479
Uh, can we do it six by eight?
High contrast.

09:17.014 --> 09:19.518
♪ Home is
Wherever I’m with you ♪

09:19.519 --> 09:21.354
[♪ uplifting music plays]

09:21.355 --> 09:22.624
[spectators cheering]

09:22.625 --> 09:23.760
Excuse me.

09:23.761 --> 09:25.129
- Hi.
- What?

09:25.130 --> 09:26.732
Where were you?
It’s about to happen.

09:26.733 --> 09:28.637
- She found a stray.
- No, no, I did not.

09:28.804 --> 09:31.877
I put a handsome, sweaty man
in an Uber and I sent him
on his way,

09:32.043 --> 09:33.914
because I am an evolved woman.

09:34.114 --> 09:36.184
Sorry, I’m here now,
and I’m ready to support
your tiny marathon.

09:36.185 --> 09:38.690
Okay, Anton, your coworkers
are 30 yards up that way.

09:38.890 --> 09:40.760
Thirty yards? That is far.

09:40.761 --> 09:42.665
- Anton!
- Sorry, sorry.

09:42.865 --> 09:44.769
Okay, and in case
they ask any questions,
here are our contingencies.

09:44.969 --> 09:47.609
For instance, if they ask you
why you don’t have
running shoes,

09:47.875 --> 09:49.779
it’s because Issa was supposed
to bring them for you,

09:49.879 --> 09:52.015
but she forgot because
her fake aunt is in town.

09:52.016 --> 09:53.485
I love that. Her name’s Bonnie
and she’s a klepto.

09:53.787 --> 09:55.825
Great. Okay, and now you two
are fake fighting,

09:56.024 --> 09:58.630
but she still showed up
to support you because
her fake therapist told her to,

09:58.763 --> 10:00.365
and Samir is
that fake therapist.

10:00.366 --> 10:01.870
No, I don’t like that one.
Can I have a different one?

10:01.970 --> 10:03.271
- No. Okay, review that.
- [sighing] Okay.

10:03.272 --> 10:04.809
- Okay.
- Okay.

10:05.243 --> 10:06.580
- [Issa gasps]
- Breast cancer awareness.

10:06.746 --> 10:07.948
- Oh, nice!
- Oh, my God.

10:07.949 --> 10:09.685
- Sweat.
- Okay, got it.

10:10.119 --> 10:11.890
Okay. Face.

10:13.359 --> 10:14.962
You guys, here I go.

10:15.129 --> 10:17.433
[♪ uplifting music playing]

10:17.434 --> 10:18.902
[breathes deeply]

10:18.903 --> 10:23.847
- [echoing] Go, Anton!
- [echoing] Yeah, Anton!

10:25.918 --> 10:27.520
[groans]

10:27.521 --> 10:28.523
[♪ music stops]

10:28.757 --> 10:30.092
[all] Oh.

10:30.093 --> 10:31.997
- [groaning]
- [Samir] And he’s got a cramp.

10:32.197 --> 10:33.566
[Billie] And he’s fine.

10:33.567 --> 10:35.201
Someone get him a banana.

10:35.202 --> 10:37.708
- Oh, my God. There he is! Yes!
- [cheering]

10:38.075 --> 10:39.143
What?

10:39.144 --> 10:41.850
You guys! No, no!

10:42.016 --> 10:44.588
Oh, my God, I’m emotional.

10:44.589 --> 10:45.991
I’m doing this for you.

10:46.091 --> 10:47.260
And breast cancer, okay?
We’re gonna fix it.

10:47.393 --> 10:49.296
- We gotta fix it.
- Yes, we can.

10:49.297 --> 10:50.567
- We love you.
We love you, buddy.
- Mwah, mwah and mwah.

10:50.568 --> 10:51.670
What is he...

10:51.936 --> 10:52.739
Anton?

10:52.938 --> 10:53.974
[coworker] Oh, my God.

10:54.174 --> 10:55.711
[coworkers cheering]

10:55.911 --> 10:57.013
[♪ music resumes]

10:57.146 --> 10:58.984
- You’re our hero!
- Yes, he is!

11:00.721 --> 11:01.723
[♪ music stops]

11:01.956 --> 11:02.925
[chattering]

11:03.092 --> 11:04.494
Okay, now he’s milking it.

11:04.795 --> 11:07.834
[coworkers] Go, Anton!
Go, Anton! Go, Anton!

11:07.835 --> 11:09.237
Oh, I love it!

11:09.337 --> 11:11.710
- Okay, here we go.
- [coworker 2] You got this. Oh.

11:11.910 --> 11:14.446
- Is he gonna do it?
- He’s gonna end up
running a marathon.

11:14.447 --> 11:16.351
Okay, bye!

11:17.287 --> 11:18.354
He’s a riot.

11:18.355 --> 11:20.694
[♪ music resumes]

11:24.936 --> 11:27.306
Now, help, please.
Help, please.

11:27.307 --> 11:29.009
[groans] Thanks.

11:29.010 --> 11:31.716
- God, I’m fake proud of him.
- I know. I’m fake proud of us.

11:35.323 --> 11:37.661
To Billie! She pulled
the whole thin -Cheers. All right.
- It worked.

11:37.662 --> 11:39.197
[Anton] Can I be honest?

11:39.198 --> 11:40.566
I now know what they mean
by runner’s high.

11:40.567 --> 11:41.970
- [Samir] Do you?
- You know what?

11:42.070 --> 11:43.473
- Your skin looks amazing.
- Right?

11:43.707 --> 11:45.108
Endorphins.

11:45.109 --> 11:46.713
Even as I sit here, the
endorphins are still with me.

11:46.980 --> 11:49.117
Mmm. Oh, will you get me
another one when you’re inside?

11:49.217 --> 11:51.590
Yep. Anything
for my favorite reporter.

11:51.823 --> 11:52.658
Okay.

11:52.825 --> 11:54.762
- Me too, please.
- Okay.

11:54.929 --> 11:57.467
Okay, wait, wait.
So what actually happened
with that fainting runner guy?

11:57.768 --> 11:59.773
I told you, I left him alone.
No more strays.

11:59.972 --> 12:01.407
- Yeah?
- Mm-hmm.

12:01.408 --> 12:03.881
I’m tired of getting hurt.
I want a guy with a job

12:04.080 --> 12:04.949
and his own place.

12:05.116 --> 12:06.653
- Okay.
- No offense.

12:06.886 --> 12:07.989
Some taken.

12:08.122 --> 12:11.864
- Hello! Hi. Hello.
- [Issa] Oh. Hi!

12:12.063 --> 12:13.298
- Oh. Uh...
- Hi.

12:13.299 --> 12:14.901
- I hope we’re not intruding.
- No.

12:14.902 --> 12:16.038
- We’re Anton’s coworkers.
- Yes.

12:16.238 --> 12:17.307
Yes, you are. [chuckles]

12:17.440 --> 12:18.810
- Yes.
- Yes, the coworkers. Yes.

12:18.977 --> 12:20.613
We wanted to see Anton again,

12:20.614 --> 12:22.450
but we couldn’t track his
little number for some reason.

12:22.451 --> 12:25.188
- For some reason.
Yeah, us too. Yeah.
- Yeah.

12:25.189 --> 12:27.127
- For a reason we don’t know.
- [Issa] Yeah. At all.

12:27.327 --> 12:30.066
We tried texting him,
but figured he’s still
out there running.

12:30.232 --> 12:31.770
Yes, yes, he is still running,

12:32.003 --> 12:34.007
because it would be way too
fast for him to have finished.

12:34.008 --> 12:35.343
- [Issa] Yes, yes.
- [Samir] Yes.

12:35.443 --> 12:37.748
Wow, I cannot believe
you guys are here.

12:37.881 --> 12:39.316
- [chuckles] We are!
- [Issa] Yeah!

12:39.317 --> 12:41.956
You’re Anton’s coworkers
and you’re here!

12:42.423 --> 12:44.228
Now! Wow!

12:44.896 --> 12:46.566
- [chuckles]
- [chuckles]

12:46.567 --> 12:48.837
[all scream]

12:49.037 --> 12:51.173
[chattering, laughing]

12:51.174 --> 12:53.914
- [coworker 3] Can we use
your bathroom?
- [Anton] Oh, my God.

12:54.114 --> 12:54.983
- Yes.
- Yeah.

12:55.149 --> 12:57.087
What else do they want from me?

12:57.253 --> 12:58.790
[♪ tense music playing]

12:58.791 --> 13:01.596
- [Billie] So the bathroom is
this way...
- Shit.

13:01.597 --> 13:06.303
...and, um,
Anton should probably be back
from the marathon soon.

13:06.304 --> 13:07.841
Yeah.

13:09.244 --> 13:11.883
[coworker] Wow, great kitchen.

13:12.016 --> 13:13.886
- [Billie] Yes!
- [Samir] Yes, yes.

13:13.887 --> 13:15.691
- Whose, uh...
Whose house is this?
- [Billie] Samir’s parents.

13:15.957 --> 13:18.095
They’re actually on an RV trip
all over the country

13:18.096 --> 13:20.265
- to the national parks.
- [Samir] Yes, yes.

13:20.266 --> 13:22.570
They just got
to Yellowstone today,

13:22.571 --> 13:24.742
and it’s, yeah... When
a 60-year-old sees Yellowstone,

13:24.743 --> 13:28.015
it just, like...
[blows raspberry] ...changes
their life, you know?

13:28.583 --> 13:30.688
We can’t tell how amazing
it was to watch Anton today,

13:30.954 --> 13:33.058
and all of it because his
housekeeper was sick. I mean...

13:33.059 --> 13:34.529
Y-Yes, you-you should go.

13:34.862 --> 13:36.464
You should go
to the bathroom, girl.

13:36.465 --> 13:38.067
[coworker 3] Uh, sure.

13:38.068 --> 13:41.809
Yes, Prosecco. Everyone want
a glass of Prosecco?

13:41.976 --> 13:44.881
- No way! No way!
- [cheering]

13:44.882 --> 13:46.084
No!

13:46.085 --> 13:47.722
- "No" what? What?
- [Billie] Yes!

13:47.955 --> 13:49.992
- [whispering] Ring.
Ring camera.
- I knew you would.

13:49.993 --> 13:51.763
- I knew you would.
- [groans]

13:51.929 --> 13:53.532
[whispering] I have to. Please.

13:53.533 --> 13:55.603
- [whispering] No. No. No.
- California pinot grigio.

13:55.604 --> 13:58.275
- [groans]
- [Billie] I think
it could be good.

13:58.844 --> 14:01.983
- [screaming]
- Way. No way.

14:05.857 --> 14:07.594
[♪ music continues]

14:21.455 --> 14:22.859
Hey! Whoa, whoa, whoa!

14:23.125 --> 14:24.963
Stop, man. What are you--
Stop, dude. What are you--

14:25.096 --> 14:27.433
- [shushing]
- [muffled grunting]

14:27.434 --> 14:28.804
Okay, okay, okay.

14:29.370 --> 14:30.539
[whispering] Who are you?

14:30.540 --> 14:32.443
Who am I? Who are you?

14:32.444 --> 14:34.348
Some girl said I could
sleep here, and then
she gave me her sweatpants,

14:34.481 --> 14:36.585
and we made out
for a little bit.

14:36.586 --> 14:37.855
Ugh. Issa.

14:38.422 --> 14:41.227
- Were you trying
to steal my thing?
- No. No, I was borrowing it.

14:41.228 --> 14:42.497
Oh.

14:42.598 --> 14:44.166
- Why?
- Because my coworkers
are downstairs,

14:44.167 --> 14:45.738
and I told them
I was running the marathon,

14:46.004 --> 14:47.641
so if I could just borrow this,
it would mean a lot to me.

14:47.642 --> 14:49.110
You lied to your coworkers
about running the marathon?

14:49.310 --> 14:50.714
Yes, yes, exactly. So...

14:50.914 --> 14:51.916
That’s bad, man.

14:52.416 --> 14:53.753
- Ow!
- Don’t do that.

14:54.053 --> 14:56.458
- That’s messed up. [screams]
- [shushing]

14:56.459 --> 14:58.429
- Fuck! [groans]
- Shut up. What is wrong?

14:58.563 --> 15:00.065
- Cramp, cramp, cramp.
- Okay, okay.

15:00.066 --> 15:01.334
- Okay, I’ll--
- My hammy, my hammy,

15:01.335 --> 15:02.437
- my hammy, my hammy.
- Your hammy.

15:02.571 --> 15:04.140
- Use your thumb.
- Use my thumb?

15:04.141 --> 15:05.408
Don’t make it weird.

15:05.409 --> 15:06.779
Okay.

15:06.780 --> 15:08.617
Ow, fuck! Sorry for swearing,
sorry for swearing.

15:08.917 --> 15:11.021
Sorry for swearing?
Literally who are you?

15:11.188 --> 15:13.458
[groaning, breathing heavily]

15:13.459 --> 15:17.265
Okay, Paul Baker. Well, if you
could just please help me out.

15:17.266 --> 15:20.507
A lot of people are invested
in this lie. My roommates spent
all day on this.

15:20.607 --> 15:22.778
Okay, I’m okay. I’m okay.
Thank you.

15:22.945 --> 15:24.380
- Thank you so much.
- Yeah.

15:24.381 --> 15:26.553
[panting] So, wait, you made
your roommates lie too?

15:26.652 --> 15:29.692
No! They lied for me, okay?
Like friends do.

15:29.693 --> 15:31.495
Don’t you have friends
who would lie for you?

15:32.731 --> 15:34.735
- No.
- Uh, you should
probably find some.

15:34.736 --> 15:37.541
It’s like a group project.
It’s actually quite creative.

15:38.944 --> 15:41.582
So, what, you guys all just
live in this house, pay rent

15:41.583 --> 15:44.019
and just make shit up
for each other?

15:44.020 --> 15:46.157
No, we don’t pay rent.

15:46.158 --> 15:48.529
- Huh. Nice.
- Oh. Okay.

15:48.630 --> 15:50.801
This is gonna work. Um...
So nice to meet you.

15:51.067 --> 15:53.371
Congrats on the marathon,
and sorry about your leg.

15:53.372 --> 15:54.575
Thank you.

15:59.518 --> 16:01.054
[dog barking]

16:01.221 --> 16:03.425
Fucking supportive coworkers.

16:04.093 --> 16:05.830
- [screams]
- [glass clinking]

16:05.831 --> 16:08.636
So, essentially,
Samir and I grew up together.

16:08.736 --> 16:10.371
And I went to college
with him, so...

16:10.372 --> 16:12.743
Yes, and that’s
where we met Anton. So...

16:12.744 --> 16:12.810
Yes, and that’s
where we met Anton. So...
- [door opens]
- [Issa] Yeah.

16:12.811 --> 16:14.681
- [door opens]
- [Issa] Yeah.

16:14.682 --> 16:16.451
And another interesting thing
that actually happened
in college

16:16.452 --> 16:18.122
is that we, you know,
we went to a dance party--

16:18.355 --> 16:20.961
No way! No way!

16:21.228 --> 16:23.432
- [cheering]
- [Anton] Oh, my...
Oh, I’m a mess!

16:23.566 --> 16:25.502
- I’m a mess. Or am I glowing?
- No.

16:25.503 --> 16:27.507
No, I’m probably a mess.
Or am I glowing?

16:27.508 --> 16:29.913
You’re a messy glow. [laughs]
I don’t know what that means.

16:30.079 --> 16:31.514
[coworker 3] Anton, you did it!

16:31.515 --> 16:33.050
I did it!

16:33.051 --> 16:36.892
As per my... my foil blanket
and my-- a lot of sweat and...

16:36.893 --> 16:39.064
- [coworker 2] Oh, yeah.
- ...and et cetera and so forth.

16:39.197 --> 16:41.536
- Wait, show us your medal.
- Oh, yes.

16:41.937 --> 16:43.172
Hmm?

16:43.840 --> 16:45.811
- [clicks tongue]
- [Anton] Medal?

16:46.278 --> 16:47.581
[clears throat] Well, I, um...

16:47.681 --> 16:49.652
[chuckling] I didn’t finish
first, guys.

16:49.752 --> 16:51.923
I mean, I was close.
I was really close.

16:52.056 --> 16:53.559
I saw the finish line,
and then...

16:53.560 --> 16:56.064
and then someone else got
there first.

16:56.699 --> 16:59.839
- Wait, doesn’t everyone get
a medal?
- Yeah, everyone gets one.

17:00.406 --> 17:01.909
- I don’t...
- [Billie stammers]

17:01.910 --> 17:04.247
- Oh, uh, you said you lost it.
- Oh, he donated it.

17:04.248 --> 17:05.884
[coworker 3] What?

17:06.451 --> 17:09.124
Wait, you-you lost it
or you donated it?

17:09.357 --> 17:12.831
Um... [stammers] Well, uh,
it’s-it’s such a funny story.

17:12.832 --> 17:14.667
Don’t laugh yet.
So, let me start. So--

17:14.668 --> 17:16.907
- So, my Aunt Bonnie is
a klepto.
- [Anton] Mm-hmm.

17:17.139 --> 17:20.345
Yes, and Issa was actually
supposed to look
after the medal,

17:20.346 --> 17:21.716
even though
they’re having a fight.

17:21.816 --> 17:23.754
- Yes, so...
- Yes, and I’m a therapist.

17:24.454 --> 17:27.025
[Issa] So, essentially, um,
Bonnie was diagnosed
when she was 14.

17:27.026 --> 17:30.600
Okay, okay, okay.
You guys, please, it’s fine.

17:32.470 --> 17:33.941
Thanks. Um...

17:34.575 --> 17:38.883
[smacks lips] My friends
are trying to protect me,
because that’s what they do,

17:38.884 --> 17:40.753
but I’m gonna be honest.

17:40.754 --> 17:43.892
I, uh... I should probably just
tell you I didn’t actually--

17:43.893 --> 17:46.498
Yo! [grunts]
Dude, hey. Dude, there you are.

17:46.666 --> 17:48.735
Hi. Sorry, I couldn’t find you.

17:48.736 --> 17:50.773
Thanks for letting me borrow
that for the picture, man.

17:50.774 --> 17:53.078
My mom’s gonna love it.

17:53.880 --> 17:58.155
My mom, who is the president

17:58.756 --> 18:02.363
of Latvia.

18:03.365 --> 18:04.734
[Anton] Of course.

18:04.735 --> 18:06.906
No problem, Paul Baker.

18:07.106 --> 18:08.309
No problem at all.

18:09.243 --> 18:12.282
- [whispering] Hi.
- So, who wants
to hold it first?

18:12.283 --> 18:15.255
- [all gasp, cheer]
- Who’s the lucky first?

18:15.256 --> 18:17.393
- Hey, no more strays?
- [stammers] Mmm?

18:17.493 --> 18:18.963
This is the last one.
This is the last one.

18:18.964 --> 18:21.602
- Oh, wow, that was amazing.
- You liked that?

18:21.603 --> 18:24.274
- [cheering, laughing]
- Oh.

18:32.023 --> 18:34.527
[♪ suspenseful m -No, no. It’s not...
It’s not happening.
- Sammy!

18:34.528 --> 18:36.331
Sammy, don’t you want to use
the front door?

18:36.498 --> 18:37.600
Won’t the front door feel good?

18:37.601 --> 18:38.970
[Anton] Yeah, Samir, come on.

18:39.137 --> 18:40.773
- Lying is stressful.
- Mm-hmm.

18:40.774 --> 18:42.376
Take it from someone who’s now
leading an office fun run.

18:42.476 --> 18:44.682
- [Billie] Wait, what?
- Type 1 diabetes.

18:44.782 --> 18:46.686
I, uh...
I actually can’t get into it.

18:46.786 --> 18:48.456
Wait, what do you mean
you don’t like running?

18:48.623 --> 18:50.660
That is boring as hell, man.

18:50.827 --> 18:54.668
I just moved to New York
a little bit ago, so I just ran
the marathon to meet people.

18:54.835 --> 18:56.705
You ran 26 miles
to meet people?

18:56.706 --> 18:58.442
Uh-huh. Yeah.

19:00.346 --> 19:01.481
And did you?

19:03.218 --> 19:04.286
Yeah, I did.

19:04.287 --> 19:06.223
- [both chuckle]
- [Billie] Come on.

19:06.224 --> 19:07.661
Come on, Samir,
it’s now or never, right?
Let’s do it now, then.

19:07.828 --> 19:09.162
- [Billie] Come on.
- Okay.

19:09.163 --> 19:10.666
- Come on.
- Okay, okay, okay, okay.

19:10.667 --> 19:12.971
- You ready?
- [Samir] Uh, sure.

19:12.972 --> 19:14.841
Okay. [smacks lips] All right.

19:14.842 --> 19:16.277
[♪ lively music plays]

19:16.278 --> 19:18.482
- [Billie chuckles]
- [Samir whimpers, sighs]

19:19.117 --> 19:21.487
[all cheering]

19:21.488 --> 19:22.658
[Billie] Good job!

19:22.824 --> 19:24.727
- Yes. Yes.
- [phone buzzing]

19:24.728 --> 19:25.930
[Issa] Yay!

19:26.632 --> 19:27.968
Fuck.

19:28.670 --> 19:31.007
Yes. Yes, Mom, I know. I know.

19:31.008 --> 19:34.848
♪♪ Alabama, Arkansas
I do love my Ma and Pa ♪

19:34.849 --> 19:38.189
♪♪ Not the way
That I do love you ♪

19:38.388 --> 19:42.495
♪ Well, holy moly, me-oh-my
You’re the apple of my eye ♪

19:42.496 --> 19:45.637
♪ Girl, I never loved one
Like you ♪

19:46.004 --> 19:47.908
♪♪ Man, oh, man
You’re my best friend ♪

19:48.208 --> 19:50.044
♪ I scream it
To the nothingness ♪

19:50.045 --> 19:52.952
♪♪ There ain’t nothing
That I need ♪

19:53.586 --> 19:55.657
♪ Well, hot
And heavy pumpkin pie ♪

19:55.757 --> 19:57.526
♪ Chocolate candy
Jesus Christ ♪

19:57.527 --> 20:00.399
♪♪ Ain’t nothing pleasing
More than you ♪

20:00.834 --> 20:04.908
♪♪ Oh, home, let me come home ♪

20:04.909 --> 20:08.516
♪♪ Home is
Wherever I’m with you ♪

20:12.758 --> 20:13.926
[♪ song ends]
